 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Cache replacement uses the 2Q eviction policy to resist sequential screening scans while retaining repeatedly compared contact sheets:
+- **FIFO probationary queue ($A1_{in}$)**: Newly rendered previews enter here on first admission. If an entry is evicted from this probationary pool without being accessed again, it is removed, preventing one-off sequential screenings from flushing frequently referenced sheets.
+- **LRU hot queue ($A_m$)**: When a requested preview is accessed again while in the cache (or re-referenced from probationary tracking), it is promoted to the hot queue, which evicts according to least-recently-used order under space pressure.
+- Total cached bytes across both queues are constrained to the 12 GiB ceiling.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache Eviction Policy | 2Q | LRU, Frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects frequently revisited comparison sheets from being evicted by long sequential screening scans, with lower lag and simpler parameter management than aged frequency counters, at the accepted cost of managing two queues and a split threshold. |
